---
import { ArrowRight, Search, Route, UsersRound, ShieldCheck } from "@lucide/astro";
---

<section class="section recommendation-next" aria-labelledby="route-demand-development-title" data-route-demand-development>
  <div class="shell">
    <div class="section-heading split-heading">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Route-demand development</p>
        <h2 id="route-demand-development-title">Use verified carrier markets to build a second source of demand beyond third-party load boards.</h2>
      </div>
      <p>
        Hermes can use verified equipment, preferred markets, historical operating patterns, direct-freight research, and public search demand to decide where route-focused business development is worth testing. The goal is to create more qualified customer, shipper, and dealer inquiries around markets a carrier can actually serve—not to promise a fixed number of loads or rankings.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="recommendation-included-grid" aria-label="Route-demand development workflow">
      <article>
        <span>01</span>
        <Route size={22} aria-hidden="true" />
        <h3>Verify the carrier pattern</h3>
        <p>Review equipment, practical capacity, preferred markets, lanes to develop or avoid, and enough operating history to separate a real pattern from a one-off move.</p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <span>02</span>
        <Search size={22} aria-hidden="true" />
        <h3>Build demand around the pattern</h3>
        <p>Research shippers, dealers, auctions, ports, manufacturers, brokers, and public search intent. Route-level SEO is published only where the evidence and canonical/indexability gates support it.</p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <span>03</span>
        <UsersRound size={22} aria-hidden="true" />
        <h3>Capture the customer request</h3>
        <p>A customer, shipper, or dealer can submit a real transportation request through the Hermes intake. The request is reviewed before any carrier match or operational commitment is made.</p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <span>04</span>
        <ShieldCheck size={22} aria-hidden="true" />
        <h3>Match only after qualification</h3>
        <p>Hermes reviews route, timing, vehicle or cargo details, equipment fit, authority, insurance, and current capacity. No page, inquiry, or search ranking automatically books freight.</p>
      </article>
    </div>

    <div class="recommendation-next-grid" style="margin-top: 2rem;">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">The operating loop</p>
        <h3>Customer / Shipper / Dealer → Hermes → qualified request → suitable carrier review.</h3>
        <p>
          The long-term advantage is not a claim that Hermes can eliminate load boards. It is a second acquisition channel designed to reduce total dependence on them by connecting direct outreach, route-aware SEO, customer intake, and the carriers already operating inside the Hermes ecosystem.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <a class="button button-primary" href="/logistics/request-vehicle-transport/#transport-intake" data-route-demand-customer-intake>
          See the customer transport intake <ArrowRight size={18} aria-hidden="true" />
        </a>
      </div>
    </div>

    <p class="recommendation-note" style="margin-top: 1.5rem;">
      <ShieldCheck size={20} aria-hidden="true" />
      Direct freight, route visibility, search rankings, inquiry volume, dedicated lanes, rates, timing, and carrier matches are not guaranteed. Historical route data is used only after review and must not expose private customer, shipment, driver, or exact live-location information.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>
